        "findings": [
          {
            "source_quote": "τίνες δὲ ἂν εἶεν κρείττονες ἢ θεοὶ μὲν ἁπάντων, ἀνθρώπων δὲ οἱ φιλόσοφοι;",
            "english_quote": "And who could be superior? The gods are superior to all, and philosophers to other human beings.",
            "issue": "The Greek presents a comparative rhetorical question: who could be superior to the gods and philosophers in the respective classes? The draft instead asks who is superior and supplies declarative answers. Although the resulting assertions reflect the hierarchy implied by the Greek, this restructuring loses the explicit comparison introduced by ἢ and changes the question’s rhetorical force. The disclosed comparative-versus-partitive ambiguity of the genitives does not require that change.",
